  You may convey verbatim copies of the Program’s source code as you receive it,
in any medium, provided that you conspicuously and appropriately publish on each copy
an appropriate copyright notice; keep intact all notices stating that this License
and any non-permissive terms added in accord with section 7 apply to the code; keep
intact all notices of the absence of any warranty; and give all recipients a copy
of this License along with the Program.

  You may charge any price or no price for each copy that you convey, and you may
offer support or warranty protection for a fee.

  5. Conveying Modified Source Versions.

  You may convey a work based on the Program, or the modifications to produce it
from the Program, in the form of source code under the terms of section 4, provided
that you also meet all of these conditions:

  a) The work must carry prominent notices stating that you modified it, and giving
a relevant date.

  b) The work must carry prominent notices stating that it is released under this
License and any conditions added under section 7. This requirement modifies the
requirement in section 4 to “keep intact all notices”.

  c) The entire work is licensed under this License, in its entirety, to any person
who comes into possession of a copy. This License must not be used to restrict licensing
of the work separately.

  d) If the work has interactive user interfaces, each must display Appropriate Legal
Notices; however, if the Program does not interact with users, this requirement does
not apply.

  These requirements apply to the combined work as a whole. If identifiable sections
of that work are derived from the Program, and can be reasonably considered independent
and separate works, then this License does not apply to those sections when you convey
them separately. But when you convey those same sections as part of a whole whose work
is based on the Program, the whole must be under this License, whose permissions for other
licensees extend to the entire whole.

  6. Conveying Non-Source Forms.

  You may convey a covered work in object code form under the terms of sections 4
and 5, provided that you also convey the machine-readable Corresponding Source under
the terms of this License, in one of these ways:

  a) Convey the object code in, or embodied in, a physical product (including a
physical distribution medium), accompanied by the Corresponding Source fixed on a durable
physical medium customarily used for software interchange.

  b) Convey the object code in, or embodied in, a physical product, accompanied by a
written offer, valid for at least three years and valid for as long as you offer spare
parts or customer support for that product model, to give anyone who possesses the object
code either (1) a copy of the Corresponding Source for all the software in the product
that is covered by this License, on a durable physical medium customarily used for software
interchange, for a price no more than your reasonable cost of physically performing this
conveying of source, or (2) access to copy the Corresponding Source from a designated
place.

  c) Convey individual copies of the object code with a copy of the written offer to
provide the Corresponding Source. This alternative is allowed only occasionally and noncommercially,
and only if you received the object code with such an offer, in accord with subsection 6b.

  d) Convey the object code by offering access from a designated place, for no charge.
You need not require recipients to copy the Corresponding Source along with the object code.
If the place to copy the object code is a network server, the Corresponding Source may be on a different server, operated by you or a third party, provided that you maintain clear instructions beside the object code telling people where to find it.

  e) Convey the object code using peer-to-peer transmission, provided you inform other
peers where the object code and Corresponding Source for the work are being offered to the
general public at no charge.

  A “User Product” is either (1) a “consumer product”, which means any tangible personal
property normally used for personal, family, or household purposes, or (2) anything designed
or sold for incorporation into a dwelling. In determining whether a product is a consumer
product, doubtful cases shall be resolved in favor of coverage. For a particular product
received by a particular user, “normally used” refers to a typical or common use of that
class of product, regardless of the particular user’s actual use.

  “Installation Information” for a User Product means any methods, procedures, authorization
keys, or other information required to install and execute modified versions of a covered work
in that User Product from a modified version of its Corresponding Source. The information must
suffice to ensure that the continued operation of that modified version does not merely continue
to operate if the installation was performed, but also that the modified work functions in all
important respects in the same way as the original version.

  When you convey an object code work under this section, you must also convey any required
Installation Information, but only to the extent that such information is necessary to install and
execute modified versions of the covered work in that User Product. If no such information is required,
you need not convey any.

