  11. Patents.

  A “contributor” is a copyright holder who authorizes use under this License of the Program or a work on which the Program is based. The work thus licensed is called the contributor’s “contributor version”.

  A contributor’s “essential patent claims” are all patent claims owned or controlled by the contributor, whether already acquired or hereafter acquired, that would be infringed by some manner, permitted by this License, of making, using, selling, offering for sale, importing, or otherwise transferring its contributor version.

  If you convey a covered work, you knowingly rely on a patent license, and the Corresponding Source of the work is not available for anyone to copy, then you must either (1) make the Corresponding Source available, or (2) arrange to deprive yourself of the benefit of the patent license for this particular conveyance, or (3) arrange, in a legally enforceable way, to make the Corresponding Source not subject to this License for that conveyance.

  “Knowingly relying” means you have actual knowledge that, but for the patent license, your conveying the covered work, in the circumstances, would infringe a patent claim of the contributor version.

  If, pursuant to or in connection with a single transaction or arrangement, you convey, or procure the conveyance of, a covered work and also grant a patent license to some of the parties receiving the covered work, then the patent license you grant is automatically extended to all recipients of the covered work and works based on the covered work.

  No patent license is granted under this License if the conveyance of the covered work does not occur under this License.

  Nothing in this section shall be construed as excluding or limiting any rights you may have under applicable patent law.

